// The target string is "Lumi", not "LUMI Keys", and the tags and insertModes
// attributes are gone.
//
// This is what was emptying the settings panel, and it is not code at all. Probes with
// the identical activation list showed a full panel while this program showed none, and
// the only thing left that differed was the metadata line. Dashboard reads that first
// to decide what the program is for; an unrecognised target and it renders nothing,
// with no error to say so.

// Per-note colour display driven over MIDI channel 16 by the LumiPaint CLAP plugin.
//
// Colour, the fast way: polyphonic aftertouch, one message per component.
//
//   channel 14, note n, value v   red   of note n
//   channel 15, note n, value v   green of note n
//   channel 16, note n, value v   blue  of note n
//
// The note is inside the message, so nothing needs selecting first and the three are
// independent. A dropped one costs a single component of a single key for one frame
// instead of putting a whole colour on the wrong note, and a sender only has to send
// the components that actually changed - a fade that moves red and leaves green and
// blue alone costs one message rather than four.
//
// The CC form below still works and is what the Falcon bridge uses.
//
//   CC 102 n   select note n for colour writes
//   CC 103 r   red,   7-bit
//   CC 104 g   green, 7-bit
//   CC 105 b   blue,  7-bit; commits and CLEARS the selection, so every colour write
//              must anchor with its own CC 102. That is deliberate: a relayed cluster
//              link can deliver a message twice as easily as it can lose one, and an
//              auto-incrementing cursor turns one duplicated commit into a stray
//              colour on the next semitone. Clearing makes a repeat a no-op.
//   CC 106 v   global brightness
//   CC 107 v   resting level for unlit keys
//   CC 108 0   clear all colours   1 clear lit keys   2 restore default wheel
//   CC 109 v   display offset in semitones, centred at 64
//   CC 110 0/1 octave folding
//   CC 111 n   light the key for note n
//   CC 112 n   unlight it
//   CC 113 r   highlight red,   7-bit
//   CC 114 g   highlight green, 7-bit
//   CC 115 b   highlight blue,  7-bit; commits the highlight colour
//   CC 116 0/1 highlight off / on
//   CC 117 r   pressed-key red,   7-bit
//   CC 118 g   pressed-key green, 7-bit
//   CC 119 b   pressed-key blue,  7-bit; commits the pressed colour
//   CC 85  0/1 pressed colour off / on   (85 because 120-127 are channel mode
//                                         messages and a DAW panic would collide)
//   CC 28  n   config item id to write next
//   CC 29  v   value for that item; writes it and pushes it round the cluster
//
// Sent OUT by this program:
//   CC 45  n   how many blocks are in the cluster
//   CC 46  n   lowest note visible on the chain, sent by the leftmost block only
//              Between them the plugin knows exactly which notes the hardware can
//              show, so it need not animate or transmit the other hundred.
//
//   CC 30  n   config item id whose value changed
//   CC 31  v   its new value
//              Together these mirror the keybed settings to the plugin, so turning a
//              knob in Dashboard or pressing an octave button is reflected there, and
//              the plugin can write them back. Signed items travel offset by 64.
//
//   CC 86      ignored. Chained units place themselves now, from getClusterXpos()
//              and getClusterWidth(), so nothing needs to tell them how far apart
//              they are. The plugin still sends it; the device drops it.
//   CC 20/21/22  pressure gradient colour r/g/b, 7-bit; 22 commits
//   CC 23/24/25  pitch bend gradient colour r/g/b, 7-bit; 25 commits
//              A held key uses its own bend from keyMove; a key lit by the host uses
//              pitch bend received on channel 16.
//   CC 26  0/1  pressure gradient off / on
//   CC 27  0/1  pitch bend gradient off / on
//   CC 51  0/1  bend trail off / on. Lights the keys between a bent note and the
//               note it is bending towards, so the gesture reads as a distance.
//   CC 43  0/1  send pitch bend from the keys, off / on
//   CC 44  0/1  send aftertouch from the keys, off / on
//   CC 47  0/1  link octaves across the chain
// Sent OUT, once per block:
//   CC 48  n   this block's position in the chain
//   CC 49  n   the lowest note this block is showing
//              Every block reports its own range, so the plugin can draw what each one
//              covers whether they are spread or mirrored., off / on. With it off each block
//               shifts on its own, which is useful when two blocks are covering
//               different registers rather than acting as one long keyboard.
//               Both still tracked when off, so the gradients keep working even when
//               the instrument is not meant to receive the data.
//
//   CC 32  n    bend full-scale, in units of 64. A key bends by Key Pitch Bend Amount
//               against Pitch Bend Range - one semitone out of 48 is ~170 of the
//               14-bit range, so scaling against the full range makes the gradient
//               almost invisible. This says how much deviation counts as maximum.
//   CC 87  n   octave, centred at 64. Absolute, and applied by every unit in the
//              chain, which is the only way they stay in step - each block runs its
//              own copy of this program and a button press only reaches one of them.
//
// Sent OUT by this program:
//
// Priority, highest first: physical press, incoming MIDI, the note's own colour. So a
// key you are holding reads as yours even if a sequencer is lighting the same note.
//
// Buttons: minus/plus shift the octave of the colour map.

int numKeys;
int startNote;
int octaveShift;
int numButtons;

// Where this block sits in a chain. Config item 8 turned out to report the same value
// on every unit, so position comes from getClusterXpos() instead: 0 for the leftmost
// block, 1 for the next, and so on. Everything in a cluster arrives on one MIDI port
// and every block sees identical messages, so this is the only thing that can tell a
// unit it is the upper half rather than a second copy of the lower one.
//
// ROLI's own formula, from StudioLightkeyScript: the shift is centred on the cluster
// and counted in octaves, doubled because a LUMI block spans two of them. A two-block
// chain therefore straddles the middle rather than starting at the left, and any
// cluster width works without being told how wide it is.
int topOctaveShift;
int clusterWidthLast;
int xPosLast;

// Verbatim from ROLI's LittleFootLibrary/ConfigIds.littlefoot.
const int ConfigId_midiStartChannel    = 0;
const int ConfigId_midiEndChannel      = 1;
const int ConfigId_midiUseMPE          = 2;
const int ConfigId_pitchBendRange      = 3;
const int ConfigId_octave              = 4;
const int ConfigId_transpose           = 5;
const int ConfigId_slideCC             = 6;
const int ConfigId_slideMode           = 7;
const int ConfigId_octaveTopology      = 8;
const int ConfigId_midiChannelRange    = 9;
const int ConfigId_velocitySensitivity = 10;
const int ConfigId_glideSensitivity    = 11;
const int ConfigId_slideSensitivity    = 12;
const int ConfigId_pressureSensitivity = 13;
const int ConfigId_liftSensitivity     = 14;
const int ConfigId_fixedVelocity       = 15;
const int ConfigId_fixedVelocityValue  = 16;
const int ConfigId_pianoMode           = 17;
const int ConfigId_glideLockRate       = 18;
const int ConfigId_glideLockEnable     = 19;
const int ConfigId_xTrackingMode       = 30;
const int ConfigId_zTrackingMode       = 32;
const int ConfigId_MPEZone             = 40;

const int ConfigId_gammaCorrection     = 33;
const int ConfigId_brightness          = 36;

int noteColour[128];
bool noteLit[128];
/* Sized to the keys that exist, not to a round number.

   These were 64 each for a 24-key block - 480 wasted slots across five arrays, nearly
   two kilobytes on a device whose heap is measured in kilobytes. Littlefoot gives no
   error when it runs short; things simply stop working, and a settings panel that
   silently fails to appear is exactly the shape that takes. */
int pressed[24];
int keyPressure[24];
int keyBend[24];

// Bend from the host, for keys lit by incoming MIDI rather than held by a finger.
int incomingBend;
int bendFullScale;

// Enable switches for bend and aftertouch output. Deliberately NOT config items: the
// user config range has no names in the firmware, so Dashboard renders items 64 and 65
// as bare numbers with no label and no clue what they do. They live in the plugin,
// which can label them.
int enablePitchBend;
int enablePressure;
int linkOctaves;

// Keybed settings mirrored to the plugin. Polled rather than hooked because Dashboard
// writes these behind the program's back.
const int numWatched = 13;
int watchedItems[numWatched];
int watchedLast[numWatched];
int pendingConfigItem;
int lastSentWidth;
int lastSentBase;

// Housekeeping runs on a slow tick, not every frame.
//
// repaint() was polling fifteen config items, querying the cluster three times and
// reading transpose once per key - about forty config reads and several cluster calls
// for every frame, in an interpreted VM. That, not the number of LEDs, is what made
// animation crawl: the frame rate collapsed and no amount of MIDI throughput could
// make up for it. None of that work changes at frame rate, so it now happens roughly
// once a second, staggered so no single frame carries all of it.
int slowTick;
int cachedTranspose;
int cachedBendRange;
int useBendTrail;

// Last octave this block saw, for spotting a change made behind the program's back.
int lastOctaveSeen;

int lastSentPos;
int lastSentLow;

// Counts pollWindow passes, so the reports repeat occasionally instead of only when
// something changes.
int repeatTick;

// Cycles through the watched items so one is re-sent on every pass.
int refreshCursor;

// Marks a block-to-block message as one of ours: handleMessage sees everything.
const int kRangeTag = 0x4C50;

const int kReportStatus = 0xA0;
const int kSlotWidth    = 100;
const int kSlotBase     = 101;
const int kSlotBlock    = 110;

void sendReport(int slot, int value) {
    sendMIDI(kReportStatus | controlChannel, slot & 0x7F, clamp(0, 127, value));
}

// Set when something arrived that changes the picture. Nothing reads it yet - the
// firmware redraws on its own schedule - but it marks the intent, and gives a place to
// hang a dirty check if the paint loop ever gets expensive enough to want one.
int pendingFrame;

int usePressureGrad;
int pressureColour;
int useBendGrad;
int bendColour;
int pendingGradRed;
int pendingGradGreen;
int pendingGradBlue;


int globalBrightness;
int unlitLevel;
int hostNoteOffset;
int foldMode;
int pitchClassLit;

int useHighlight;
int highlightColour;
int pendingHiRed;
int pendingHiGreen;
int pendingHiBlue;

int usePress;
int pressColour;
int pendingPrRed;
int pendingPrGreen;
int pendingPrBlue;

int controlChannel;
int midiNoteOff;
int midiNoteOn;
int midiCC;

int expand7(int v) {
    return ((v << 1) | (v >> 6)) & 0xFF;
}

int defaultColour(int pc) {
    if (pc == 0)  { return 0xFFFF2B2B; }
    if (pc == 1)  { return 0xFFFF7A1F; }
    if (pc == 2)  { return 0xFFFFC400; }
    if (pc == 3)  { return 0xFFD6FF00; }
    if (pc == 4)  { return 0xFF5CFF00; }
    if (pc == 5)  { return 0xFF00FF5C; }
    if (pc == 6)  { return 0xFF00FFD6; }
    if (pc == 7)  { return 0xFF00C4FF; }
    if (pc == 8)  { return 0xFF007AFF; }
    if (pc == 9)  { return 0xFF2B2BFF; }
    if (pc == 10) { return 0xFF9C00FF; }
    return 0xFFFF00B8;
}

void resetColours() {
    for (int i = 0; i < 128; i = i + 1) {
        noteColour[i] = defaultColour(i % 12);
    }
}

void clearColours() {
    for (int i = 0; i < 128; i = i + 1) {
        noteColour[i] = 0xFF000000;
    }
}

void clearLit() {
    for (int i = 0; i < 128; i = i + 1) {
        noteLit[i] = false;
    }
}

// Base 48, as in ROLI's script. The octave is shared across the cluster; the topology
// shift is this block's own place in it.
// Each block always takes its own place in the chain. Whether the blocks shift
// together is a separate question, answered by linkOctaves.
int getBaseNoteNumber() {
    return startNote + ((octaveShift + topOctaveShift) * 12);
}

bool isSignedConfig(int item) {
    return item == ConfigId_octave || item == ConfigId_transpose;
}

int encodeConfigValue(int item, int value) {
    if (isSignedConfig(item)) { return clamp(0, 127, value + 64); }
    return clamp(0, 127, value);
}

int decodeConfigValue(int item, int value) {
    if (isSignedConfig(item)) { return value - 64; }
    return value;
}

void sendConfigItemToCluster(int itemId) {
    int n = getNumBlocksInCurrentCluster();

    if (n < 2) { return; }

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) {
        if (getBlockIdForBlockInCluster(i) != getBlockIDForIndex(0)) {
            setRemoteConfig(getBlockIdForBlockInCluster(i), itemId, getLocalConfig(itemId));
        }
    }
}

// Blocks share the octave between themselves with setRemoteConfig, the way ROLI's
// ClusterSync library does. No host round trip, so the chain stays in step even with
// nothing connected - which the previous CC 88 to the plugin and back could not do.
// Reported on change rather than polled by the plugin, so adding or removing a block,
// or shifting octave, updates the window immediately.
/*
    This block tells the plugin where it sits and what it is showing.

    Sent whether the blocks are linked or not, which is the point: when they are
    independent each one is somewhere different, and that is exactly when seeing both
    ranges matters. Reported on change rather than on a timer, so the editor follows an
    octave button as it is pressed.
*/
/*
    This block tells the plugin where it sits.

    Only one block in a chain is connected to the host, so a chained block calling
    sendCC is talking to nobody - which is why the second block's bracket never
    appeared however often it announced itself. It does have a route to its neighbours
    though, over the connector, so it hands its position to the master and the master
    passes it on.

    Tagged, because handleMessage sees anything any block sends and there is no other
    way to tell whose message this is.
*/
void reportRange() {
    int myPos = getClusterXpos();
    int myLow = clamp(0, 127, getDisplayNote(0));

    if (myPos == lastSentPos && myLow == lastSentLow) { return; }

    lastSentPos = myPos;
    lastSentLow = myLow;

    /* Both routes, every time, from every block.

       Deciding first which block can reach the host was the mistake: whichever way
       that guess went, one block reported and the other did not, and with the blocks
       unlinked neither did. A CC from a block with no route to the host simply goes
       nowhere - it costs two messages and harms nothing - so there is no reason to
       gamble on being the right block. Send it directly in case this is the one that
       is heard, and hand it to the neighbours in case it is not. */
    sendReport(kSlotBlock + myPos, myLow);

    int n = getNumBlocksInCurrentCluster();

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) {
        if (getBlockIdForBlockInCluster(i) != getBlockIDForIndex(0)) {
            sendMessageToBlock(getBlockIdForBlockInCluster(i), kRangeTag, myPos, myLow);
        }
    }
}

/* A neighbour's range, relayed on to the host. Ignored unless this is the block that
   can reach it, so the message going to every block in the cluster is harmless. */
void handleMessage(int data1, int data2, int data3) {
    if (data1 != kRangeTag) { return; }

    /* Relayed without checking whether this block is the one the host listens to. If
       it is not, the two messages go nowhere and nothing is lost; if it is, the
       neighbour's position gets through. Guessing wrong here silences a block, and
       being wrong costs nothing. */
    sendReport(kSlotBlock + data2, data3);
}

void pollWindow() {
    /*
        Say it again every so often, not only when it changes.

        Everything here was sent on change alone, which assumes someone was listening
        at the moment it changed. Nobody always is: the plugin reconnects, the editor
        opens later, another instance takes the keyboard, the host is started after the
        keyboard. Miss that one announcement and the plugin never learns there is a
        second block at all - it had been told once, hours ago, with nothing plugged in.

        pollWindow runs about once a second, so repeating every eighth pass costs three
        messages every eight seconds and removes a whole class of "it worked yesterday".
    */
    repeatTick = repeatTick + 1;

    bool sayAgain = false;

    if (repeatTick >= 8) {
        repeatTick = 0;
        sayAgain = true;
    }

    int w = getClusterWidth();

    if (w < 1) { w = 1; }

    if (w != lastSentWidth || sayAgain) {
        lastSentWidth = w;
        sendReport(kSlotWidth, w);
    }

    /* The periodic re-report, restored.

       It was removed on the suspicion that repeating a position pulled the blocks back
       together. That was the wrong culprit: the real cause was an ungated push in
       readTopology, which is fixed. A range report moves nothing - it only tells the
       plugin where a block already is - and without repeating it, the bracket appears
       only if the plugin happened to be listening at the moment the block moved. */
    if (sayAgain) { lastSentPos = 0 - 1; }

    reportRange();

    // Only the leftmost block reports the start, or the blocks would take turns
    // sending different values for it.
    if (getClusterXpos() == 0) {
        int b = getDisplayNote(0);

        if (b < 0) { b = 0; }
        if (b > 127) { b = 127; }

        if (b != lastSentBase || sayAgain) {
            lastSentBase = b;
            sendReport(kSlotBase, b);
        }
    }
}

/*
    Settings that changed, and occasionally one that did not.

    Reporting only on change assumes the one announcement gets through. It usually does,
    but not always: a report is polyphonic aftertouch carrying a slot number, and the
    plugin has to tell those apart from real key pressure, which it does by ignoring any
    that concern a note currently held. Slots 30 and 32 are watched config items and are
    also notes F#0 and G#0 - hold one of those and a genuine report for that slot is
    read as pressure and dropped, permanently, because it is never sent again.

    So one item is re-sent on each pass, cycling through them. Thirteen items at about a
    second a pass means anything missed comes back within fifteen seconds, without
    adding meaningful traffic. Reporting a value that has not changed costs nothing at
    the other end - the plugin compares before acting.
*/
void pollWatchedConfig() {
    for (int i = 0; i < numWatched; i = i + 1) {
        int v = getLocalConfig(watchedItems[i]);

        if (v != watchedLast[i]) {
            watchedLast[i] = v;
            sendReport(watchedItems[i], encodeConfigValue(watchedItems[i], v));
        }
    }

    refreshCursor = refreshCursor + 1;

    if (refreshCursor >= numWatched) { refreshCursor = 0; }

    sendReport(watchedItems[refreshCursor],
               encodeConfigValue(watchedItems[refreshCursor],
                                 getLocalConfig(watchedItems[refreshCursor])));
}

/*
    Watches the octave for a change and pushes it round the cluster.

    Modelled on ROLI's ClusterSync, which polls getLocalConfig(ConfigId_octave) from
    repaint() every frame and never touches the octave buttons itself. That is the part
    I had wrong: on a LUMI those buttons are handled in firmware, which writes config
    item 4 directly - so handleButtonDown never fires, setOctave never runs, and each
    block quietly shifted on its own with nothing to tell the others.

    Polling catches the change whoever made it: the buttons, Dashboard, the plugin, or a
    setRemoteConfig from the other block. One config read a frame, which is what ROLI
    spends too.

    Both routes out, as before: setRemoteConfig over the chain connector, and a report
    to the plugin which broadcasts it back over MIDI to every block.
*/
/*
    The link toggle lives in the plugin, not in a config item.

    It was briefly kept in config item 8, octaveTopology, so that Dashboard could show
    it too. That was a mistake: ROLI never activate item 8 anywhere - ClusterSync skips
    it explicitly and the LUMI script does not mention it - and activating it stopped
    Dashboard showing any of this program's settings at all. A derived item is derived
    for a reason.

    There is no firmware-named item that means "link the octaves", and a user item
    shows in Dashboard as a bare number with no label, which is worse than not being
    there. So it stays on CC 47 and lives in the plugin.
*/

void pollOctave() {
    /* Only meaningful while linked: unlinked, the octave does not live in the config
       item and nothing there should be acted on. */
    if (linkOctaves == 0) { return; }

    int now = getLocalConfig(ConfigId_octave);

    if (now == lastOctaveSeen) { return; }

    lastOctaveSeen = now;
    octaveShift = now;
    watchedLast[3] = now;

    // The range moved, so say so - independent blocks especially, since nothing else
    // is going to mention it.
    reportRange();

    /* Always tell the plugin, linked or not: the editor should follow wherever a block
       has gone, and that matters most when they are independent. */
    sendReport(ConfigId_octave, now + 64);

    /* No push to the cluster from here.

       This only sees changes made somewhere else - Dashboard, or a linked block that
       already sent it to us. Pushing it onward would bounce it back to where it came
       from, and with the blocks unlinked would share an octave the toggle says should
       not be shared. The buttons announce their own changes in setOctave. */
}

void readTopology() {
    int newShift = 0;

    if (getClusterWidth() > 1) {
        int octStart = (getClusterWidth() - 1) / 2;
        newShift = (getClusterXpos() - octStart) * 2;
    }

    topOctaveShift = newShift;

    if (clusterWidthLast != getClusterWidth() || xPosLast != getClusterXpos()) {
        clusterWidthLast = getClusterWidth();
        xPosLast = getClusterXpos();

        /* Only when the blocks are meant to share an octave.

           This push comes from ROLI's ClusterSync, where sharing is unconditional
           because their program has no notion of independent blocks. Ours does, and
           this was the one place left that pushed the octave round the cluster without
           asking the toggle - so any time the topology looked like it had changed, the
           blocks were pulled back together however the toggle was set. It is also the
           least obvious of the three, because it fires on topology rather than on
           anything the player did. */
        if (linkOctaves != 0
            && ((getClusterXpos() == 0 && !isMasterInCurrentCluster())
                || (isMasterInCurrentCluster() && isMasterBlock()))) {
            sendConfigItemToCluster(ConfigId_octave);
        }
    }
}

int getNoteForKey(int index) {
    return clamp(0, 127, getBaseNoteNumber() + index + cachedTranspose);
}

// Transpose is a keybed setting the factory panel exposes, so the notes sent and the
// notes lit both have to honour it or the lights stop matching what is played.
int getTranspose() {
    return cachedTranspose;
}

int getDisplayNote(int index) {
    return getBaseNoteNumber() + index + getTranspose() + hostNoteOffset;
}

// Idempotent: the selection is cleared once used, so a duplicated commit writes
// nothing rather than bleeding the colour onto the following note.

void handleCommand(int value) {
    if (value == 0) { clearColours(); }
    else if (value == 1) { clearLit(); }
    else if (value == 2) { resetColours(); }
}

// Returns true when the message changed something visible, which triggers a repaint.
// Colour writes return false and rely on the firmware's frame-rate repaint. Painting
// on every commit instead was tried and made things worse - only the first key of a
// sweep updated at all - so something about repainting from inside handleMIDI costs
// more than it saves. probe_blink.littlefoot exists to find out which half is at
// fault before guessing again.
/* From the plugin. Applies it without announcing it back, and only writes the config
   item when linked - the same rule as the buttons. */
void setOctaveFromHost(int o) {
    if (o < -4) { o = -4; }
    if (o > 6) { o = 6; }

    octaveShift = o;

    if (linkOctaves == 0) { return; }

    lastOctaveSeen = o;
    watchedLast[3] = o;
    setLocalConfig(ConfigId_octave, o);
}

/* One colour component of one note, from a self-describing message. */
void setComponent(int note, int which, int value) {
    if (note < 0 || note > 127) { return; }

    int v = expand7(value);
    int c = noteColour[note];

    if (which == 0) { c = (c & 0xFF00FFFF) | (v << 16); }
    else if (which == 1) { c = (c & 0xFFFF00FF) | (v << 8); }
    else { c = (c & 0xFFFFFF00) | v; }

    noteColour[note] = 0xFF000000 | (c & 0x00FFFFFF);
}

bool handleControlChange(int controller, int value) {
    /* The four-message colour write is gone.

       It existed so anything that could send MIDI could paint a note, and the Falcon
       bridge was the only thing that used it. Colours now arrive as polyphonic
       aftertouch, one self-describing message per component, which is faster and
       cannot land on the wrong key. Keeping a second way in meant carrying the
       selection state that caused this project's first bug, on a device whose spare
       memory turned out to matter. */
    if (controller == 106) { globalBrightness = expand7(value); return true; }
    else if (controller == 107) { unlitLevel = expand7(value); return true; }
    else if (controller == 108) { handleCommand(value); return true; }
    else if (controller == 109) { hostNoteOffset = value - 64; return true; }
    else if (controller == 110) { foldMode = value; return true; }
    else if (controller == 113) { pendingHiRed = expand7(value); return false; }
    else if (controller == 114) { pendingHiGreen = expand7(value); return false; }
    else if (controller == 115) {
        pendingHiBlue = expand7(value);
        highlightColour = 0xFF000000 | (pendingHiRed << 16) | (pendingHiGreen << 8) | pendingHiBlue;
        return true;
    }
    else if (controller == 116) { useHighlight = value; return true; }
    else if (controller == 117) { pendingPrRed = expand7(value); return false; }
    else if (controller == 118) { pendingPrGreen = expand7(value); return false; }
    else if (controller == 119) {
        pendingPrBlue = expand7(value);
        pressColour = 0xFF000000 | (pendingPrRed << 16) | (pendingPrGreen << 8) | pendingPrBlue;
        return true;
    }
    else if (controller == 85) { usePress = value; return true; }
    else if (controller == 20) { pendingGradRed = expand7(value); return false; }
    else if (controller == 21) { pendingGradGreen = expand7(value); return false; }
    else if (controller == 22) {
        pendingGradBlue = expand7(value);
        pressureColour = (pendingGradRed << 16) | (pendingGradGreen << 8) | pendingGradBlue;
        return true;
    }
    else if (controller == 23) { pendingGradRed = expand7(value); return false; }
    else if (controller == 24) { pendingGradGreen = expand7(value); return false; }
    else if (controller == 25) {
        pendingGradBlue = expand7(value);
        bendColour = (pendingGradRed << 16) | (pendingGradGreen << 8) | pendingGradBlue;
        return true;
    }
    else if (controller == 26) { usePressureGrad = value; return true; }
    else if (controller == 27) { useBendGrad = value; return true; }
    else if (controller == 32) { if (value > 0) { bendFullScale = value * 64; } return true; }
    else if (controller == 43) { enablePitchBend = value; return true; }
    else if (controller == 44) { enablePressure = value; return true; }
    else if (controller == 51) { useBendTrail = value; return true; }
    else if (controller == 47) { linkOctaves = value; return true; }
    // From the plugin, so apply it and stay quiet - it already knows.
    else if (controller == 87) { setOctaveFromHost(value - 64); return true; }
    else if (controller == 28) { pendingConfigItem = value; return false; }
    else if (controller == 29) {
        if (pendingConfigItem >= 0) {
            int item = pendingConfigItem;
            pendingConfigItem = -1;
            setLocalConfig(item, decodeConfigValue(item, value));

            /* Settings are shared across the chain - MPE, sensitivity, tracking - and
               that is wanted: one keyboard should feel the same all the way along. The
               octave is the exception, because it is the one thing the toggle says the
               blocks may disagree about. */
            if (item != ConfigId_octave || linkOctaves != 0) {
                sendConfigItemToCluster(item);
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}

void handleMIDI(int byte0, int byte1, int byte2) {
    int mChannel = byte0 & 0x0F;
    int status = (byte0 >> 4) & 0x0F;

    /* Polyphonic aftertouch on the top three channels is one colour component of one
       note. Checked first because during animation it is nearly all the traffic there
       is, and because these messages carry their own note number - nothing has to be
       selected beforehand and they can arrive in any order. */
    if (status == 0xA) {
        if (mChannel == 13) { setComponent(byte1, 0, byte2); return; }
        if (mChannel == 14) { setComponent(byte1, 1, byte2); return; }
        if (mChannel == 15) { setComponent(byte1, 2, byte2); return; }
    }

    if (mChannel != controlChannel) { return; }

    bool visible = false;

    if (status == midiCC) {
        visible = handleControlChange(byte1, byte2);
    }
    else if (status == 0xE) {
        incomingBend = (byte2 << 7) | byte1;
        visible = true;
    }
    else if (status == midiNoteOn && byte2 > 0) {
        noteLit[byte1] = true;
        visible = true;
    }
    else if (status == midiNoteOff || (status == midiNoteOn && byte2 == 0)) {
        noteLit[byte1] = false;
        visible = true;
    }

    /* No repaint from here.

       The firmware calls repaint() on its own at frame rate; calling it again for every
       message meant a full 24-key redraw per colour write - up to two thousand redraws
       a second against the twenty-five that were needed. That is what made animation
       crawl however little traffic was sent, and why the editor and the hardware
       disagreed: the device was spending all its time redrawing the same frame.

       A change now shows on the next frame instead of immediately, which is at most
       forty milliseconds and not perceptible. */
    if (visible) { pendingFrame = 1; }
}

// We send the MIDI ourselves, as ROLI's own LUMI script does: note on, aftertouch,
// note off, with assignChannel honouring the MPE and channel-range settings.
//
// keyMove is the bend callback - sliding along a key. Taking setUseDefaultKeyHandler
// away from the firmware means nothing sends bend unless this does.
int keyChannel[24];
int keyNote[24];

void keyStrike(int index, int z, int velocity) {
    int note = getNoteForKey(index);
    int chan = assignChannel(note);

    keyChannel[index] = chan;
    keyNote[index] = note;
    pressed[index] = 1;
    keyPressure[index] = 0;
    keyBend[index] = 8192;

    sendNoteOn(chan, note, velocity);
}

void keyPress(int index, int z, int velocity) {
    if (keyChannel[index] < 0) { return; }

    int pressure = clamp(0, 127, z);
    keyPressure[index] = pressure;

    // Still tracked when disabled, so the pressure gradient keeps working even if you
    // do not want the instrument receiving aftertouch.
    if (enablePressure != 0) {
        sendAftertouch(keyChannel[index], keyNote[index], pressure);
    }
}

void keyMove(int index, int pitchbend, int aftertouch) {
    if (keyChannel[index] < 0) { return; }

    keyBend[index] = pitchbend;
    keyPressure[index] = clamp(0, 127, aftertouch);

    if (enablePitchBend != 0) {
        sendPitchBend(keyChannel[index], pitchbend);
    }
}

void keyLift(int index, int z, int velocity) {
    if (keyChannel[index] >= 0) {
        // Recentre before releasing the channel, or the next note assigned to it
        // inherits this one's bend.
        sendPitchBend(keyChannel[index], 8192);
        sendNoteOff(keyChannel[index], keyNote[index], velocity);
        deassignChannel(keyNote[index], keyChannel[index]);
    }

    keyChannel[index] = -1;
    keyNote[index] = -1;
    pressed[index] = 0;
    keyPressure[index] = 0;
    keyBend[index] = 8192;
}

// Shift locally for immediate feedback and so the buttons still do something with no
// plugin attached, then tell the plugin, which broadcasts the absolute value to every
// unit. The local step and the broadcast agree, so the pressed unit does not move twice.
// Writing config item 4 is what keeps the notes and the lights together: the
// firmware's key handler reads it to decide which note a key sends, and the display
// below reads the same variable. Setting one without the other is how they drift.
// Applies an octave without telling anyone. Used when the value arrived from outside,
// so that acting on it does not bounce it straight back.
void applyOctave(int o) {
    if (o < -4) { o = -4; }
    if (o > 6) { o = 6; }

    octaveShift = o;
    setLocalConfig(ConfigId_octave, o);
    watchedLast[3] = o;
    lastOctaveSeen = o;
}

/*
    Announces an octave change the moment it happens.

    Two routes, because either can fail on its own. setRemoteConfig reaches the other
    blocks directly over the chain connector. The report on CC 30/31 reaches the plugin,
    which broadcasts the value back to every block over MIDI - and every block in a
    cluster receives the same MIDI, so that route works even when the connector one does
    not.

    Pushed here rather than left to the config watcher. The watcher runs about once a
    second now that housekeeping is off the frame path, so a button press took up to a
    second to reach the plugin and only then reached the other block. Announcing it at
    the moment of the press makes the two move together.
*/
/*
    Moves this block's octave and says so.

    The buttons are this program's to handle: a probe showed that pressing one reaches
    handleButtonDown and leaves config item 4 untouched, so the firmware does nothing
    with them and syncs nothing between blocks. An earlier version of this function
    only wrote the config and waited for the poll to notice, on the assumption that the
    firmware was the one writing it. It is not, so that indirection did nothing but add
    a frame of delay and a way to go wrong.

    Applied here, immediately, and announced. Whether the other blocks follow is the
    only thing the link toggle decides.
*/
/*
    Moves this block's octave.

    The octave is kept in this program's own variable and NOT written to config item 4
    unless the blocks are meant to be linked.

    That is the whole fix. Item 4 is a clustered setting: writing it propagates to the
    sibling blocks in firmware, underneath anything this program does, so while the
    octave lived there the blocks could never be independent however carefully the
    sharing was gated. Every previous attempt gated the wrong thing - the sending -
    while the storing was what tied them.

    The cost is that Dashboard's Octave field no longer follows the buttons when the
    blocks are independent, because that field is item 4 and item 4 is exactly what
    cannot be used. Linked, it is written and Dashboard follows as before.
*/
void setOctave(int o) {
    if (o < -4) { o = -4; }
    if (o > 6) { o = 6; }

    octaveShift = o;

    // The plugin always hears about it, so the editor follows the buttons either way.
    sendReport(ConfigId_octave, o + 64);
    reportRange();

    if (linkOctaves == 0) { return; }

    // Linked: the config item is the sharing mechanism, so now it is wanted.
    lastOctaveSeen = o;
    watchedLast[3] = o;
    setLocalConfig(ConfigId_octave, o);
    sendConfigItemToCluster(ConfigId_octave);
}

// No echo to the plugin here. setOctave writes config item 4, and the watcher mirrors
// that out on CC 30/31 like any other setting - reporting it twice would give the
// plugin two counters for one value, and they would drift apart.
// Button 1 is octave down, button 2 is octave up. Confirmed by probe rather than
// assumed: both reach here, and neither is handled in firmware.
void handleButtonDown(int index) {
    if (index == 1) { setOctave(octaveShift - 1); }
    else if (index == 2) { setOctave(octaveShift + 1); }
}

void updatePitchClassLit() {
    pitchClassLit = 0;

    if (foldMode == 0) { return; }

    for (int i = 0; i < 128; i = i + 1) {
        if (noteLit[i]) { pitchClassLit = pitchClassLit | (1 << (i % 12)); }
    }
}

void repaint() {
    // One config read, every frame, the way ROLI's does it. The octave has to be
    // noticed at once or the blocks visibly lag each other.
    pollOctave();

    slowTick = slowTick + 1;

    if (slowTick >= 24) { slowTick = 0; }

    // Spread across three different frames rather than landing together.
    if (slowTick == 0) {
        cachedTranspose = getLocalConfig(ConfigId_transpose);
        cachedBendRange = getLocalConfig(ConfigId_pitchBendRange);
        readTopology();
    }
    else if (slowTick == 8) {
        pollWatchedConfig();
    }
    else if (slowTick == 16) {
        pollWindow();
    }

    updatePitchClassLit();

    for (int k = 0; k < numKeys; k = k + 1) {
        int note = getDisplayNote(k);

        if (note < 0 || note > 127) {
            fillPixel(0xFF000000, k, 0);
        }
        else {
            bool lit = noteLit[note];

            if (!lit && foldMode != 0) {
                if ((pitchClassLit & (1 << (note % 12))) != 0) { lit = true; }
            }

            int col = noteColour[note];
            if (lit && useHighlight != 0) { col = highlightColour; }
            if (pressed[k] != 0 && usePress != 0) { col = pressColour; }

            // Layer order, lowest first:
            //   1. the key's own colour, or the highlight if lit, or the pressed
            //      colour if held - decided above
            //   2. pressure, mixed in by how hard the key is held
            //   3. bend on top of that result, so it reads over pressure rather than
            //      competing with it, and reaches the bend colour outright at full
            //      deflection
            if (pressed[k] != 0 && usePressureGrad != 0) {
                int pa = keyPressure[k] * 2;
                if (pa > 255) { pa = 255; }
                col = blendARGB(col, (pa << 24) | pressureColour);
            }

            /*
                The bend trail.

                A bent note is heading somewhere, and how far is the whole point of the
                gesture - but a colour on the key you are holding says nothing about
                where it is going. Lighting every key between the note and its target
                turns the bend into a distance you can see: a semitone lights one key,
                a wide bend reaches across an octave.

                How far a full bend travels is the keyboard's own Pitch Bend Range, read
                from config rather than assumed, so the trail always ends on the note
                that will actually sound.
            */
            int trailAlpha = 0;

            if (useBendTrail != 0 && cachedBendRange > 0) {
                for (int p = 0; p < numKeys; p = p + 1) {
                    if (pressed[p] == 0) { continue; }

                    int semis = ((keyBend[p] - 8192) * cachedBendRange) / 8192;

                    if (semis == 0) { continue; }

                    int target = p + semis;
                    int lo = p;
                    int hi = target;

                    if (target < p) { lo = target; hi = p; }
                    if (k < lo) { continue; }
                    if (k > hi) { continue; }

                    // Brightest at the note being held, fading towards the target.
                    int span = hi - lo;
                    int d = k - p;

                    if (d < 0) { d = 0 - d; }

                    int a = 255 - ((d * 200) / (span + 1));

                    if (a > trailAlpha) { trailAlpha = a; }
                }

                if (trailAlpha > 0) {
                    col = blendARGB(col, (trailAlpha << 24) | bendColour);
                }
            }

            int bendAlpha = 0;

            if (useBendGrad != 0 && (pressed[k] != 0 || lit)) {
                int bendValue = incomingBend;
                if (pressed[k] != 0) { bendValue = keyBend[k]; }

                int d = bendValue - 8192;
                if (d < 0) { d = 0 - d; }

                bendAlpha = (d * 255) / bendFullScale;
                if (bendAlpha > 255) { bendAlpha = 255; }

                col = blendARGB(col, (bendAlpha << 24) | bendColour);
            }

            int level = unlitLevel;
            if (pressed[k] != 0 || lit) { level = 255; }

            int alpha = (level * globalBrightness) / 255;

            // Bend lifts the key past the global brightness ceiling rather than
            // sitting under it. Recolouring alone could never make bend look brighter
            // than everything else, because the result was scaled down with the rest;
            // at full deflection a key now goes to full output whatever brightness is
            // set to.
            if (bendAlpha > 0) {
                alpha = alpha + (((255 - alpha) * bendAlpha) / 255);
                if (alpha > 255) { alpha = 255; }
            }

            // The trail lifts its keys out of the unlit level too, or a bend reaching
            // into dark keys would leave no visible trace on them.
            if (trailAlpha > alpha) { alpha = trailAlpha; }

            // Brightness is applied to the colour itself, not to the alpha channel.
            // fillPixel's alpha governs how a pixel blends with what is already in
            // the frame - it is not an LED dimmer - so encoding brightness there did
            // nothing at all, and the keys came out at full whatever Brightness and
            // Unlit level were set to. Scaling the components is what dims an LED.
            int rr = (((col >> 16) & 0xFF) * alpha) / 255;
            int gg = (((col >> 8) & 0xFF) * alpha) / 255;
            int bb = ((col & 0xFF) * alpha) / 255;

            fillPixel(0xFF000000 | (rr << 16) | (gg << 8) | bb, k, 0);
        }
    }
}

void initialise() {
    // (touch, lighting). Touch back to the firmware so MPE, channel range, velocity
    // curves, aftertouch and glide all behave as they do on the factory preset and
    // follow the Dashboard settings. Lighting stays ours.
    setUseDefaultKeyHandler(false);
    setPowerSavingEnabled(false);

    // The factory program's own settings panel, item by item:
    //
    //   Sensitivity     Strike / Pressure / Lift curves, Fixed Velocity and its value
    //   MIDI Settings   MIDI Mode, MIDI Channel, Pitch Bend Range, Key Pitch Bend Amount
    //   Pitch           Octave, Transpose
    //   Keybed State    Pitch Bend Tracking, Pressure Tracking
    //   Colors          Brightness
    //
    // Left out on purpose, because this program takes them over rather than lacking
    // them: Scale, Key, Global Key Color and Root Key Color drive the factory
    // lighting, and every colour here comes from the host instead - enabling them
    // would put controls in Dashboard that do nothing. The Mode 1-4 panels are the
    // factory program's four stored presets, which is its own feature, not a keybed
    // setting. Slide is a Seaboard axis and these keys do not have it.
    /*
        The settings panel.

        ROLI's own six from StudioLightkeyScript first, then the keybed settings their
        script has no use for but a keyboard obviously does: the three sensitivity
        curves, fixed velocity, pitch bend range, transpose and piano mode. That is the
        panel the factory program shows.

        Item 8, octaveTopology, is not touched at all - not activated, and not written.
        ROLI never go near it, it is derived rather than chosen, and both activating it
        and writing to it empty the panel completely. A probe with this exact activation
        list and nothing else produces a full panel; adding a single write to item 8 is
        what takes it away.

        Gamma is set rather than activated, as ROLI do it: a value the program chooses,
        not a control to offer.
    */
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_midiStartChannel, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_midiEndChannel, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_midiUseMPE, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_octave, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_midiChannelRange, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_MPEZone, true, true);

    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_pitchBendRange, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_transpose, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_pianoMode, true, true);

    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_velocitySensitivity, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_glideSensitivity, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_pressureSensitivity, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_liftSensitivity, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_fixedVelocity, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_fixedVelocityValue, true, true);

    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_xTrackingMode, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_zTrackingMode, true, true);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(ConfigId_brightness, true, true);


    setLocalConfigItemRange(ConfigId_octave, -4, 6);
    setLocalConfigItemRange(ConfigId_xTrackingMode, 1, 4);
    setLocalConfigItemRange(ConfigId_zTrackingMode, 1, 4);

    setLocalConfig(ConfigId_gammaCorrection, 1);

    // 24: LUMI Keys is two octaves, C to B. Getting this wrong does more than leave
    // keys unpainted - the paint loop would write past the end of the block.
    numKeys = 24;
    startNote = 48;
    numButtons = 5;
    octaveShift = getLocalConfig(ConfigId_octave);
    topOctaveShift = 0;
    clusterWidthLast = 1;
    xPosLast = 0;

    controlChannel = 15;
    midiNoteOff = 0x8;
    midiNoteOn = 0x9;
    midiCC = 0xB;

    globalBrightness = 255;
    // Half. The plugin sets this anyway on connect, but a keyboard that has been
    // flashed and not yet spoken to should show its colours, not a dark rainbow.
    unlitLevel = 128;
    hostNoteOffset = 0;
    foldMode = 0;
    pitchClassLit = 0;


    useHighlight = 0;
    highlightColour = 0xFFFFFFFF;
    pendingHiRed = 255;
    pendingHiGreen = 255;
    pendingHiBlue = 255;

    usePress = 0;
    pressColour = 0xFFFF8000;
    pendingPrRed = 255;
    pendingPrGreen = 128;
    pendingPrBlue = 0;

    readTopology();
    resetColours();
    clearLit();

    usePressureGrad = 0;
    pressureColour = 0xFFFFFF;
    useBendGrad = 0;
    bendColour = 0x00C4FF;
    pendingGradRed = 0;
    pendingGradGreen = 0;
    pendingGradBlue = 0;
    incomingBend = 8192;
    bendFullScale = 128;

    enablePitchBend = 1;
    enablePressure = 1;
    linkOctaves = 1;
    pendingConfigItem = -1;
    lastSentWidth = 0 - 1;
    lastSentBase = 0 - 1;
    slowTick = 0;
    pendingFrame = 0;
    cachedTranspose = getLocalConfig(ConfigId_transpose);
    cachedBendRange = getLocalConfig(ConfigId_pitchBendRange);
    useBendTrail = 0;
    lastOctaveSeen = getLocalConfig(ConfigId_octave);
    linkOctaves = 1;
    lastSentPos = 0 - 1;
    lastSentLow = 0 - 1;
    repeatTick = 0;
    refreshCursor = 0;

    /* Only items that are actually active, or the poll reads settings that do not
       exist and reports noise to the plugin. */
    watchedItems[0] = ConfigId_midiStartChannel;
    watchedItems[1] = ConfigId_midiEndChannel;
    watchedItems[2] = ConfigId_midiUseMPE;
    watchedItems[3] = ConfigId_octave;
    watchedItems[4] = ConfigId_midiChannelRange;
    watchedItems[5] = ConfigId_zTrackingMode;

    /* Watched but not activated, which is allowed: activation decides whether
       Dashboard offers a control, not whether the setting exists. Both still hold
       their firmware values and both are still read here - pitch bend range is what
       the plugin needs to work out where a bent note is heading, and transpose still
       moves the notes and the lights together. They simply cannot be changed from
       Dashboard while activating them takes the whole panel down. */
    watchedItems[6] = ConfigId_pitchBendRange;
    watchedItems[7] = ConfigId_transpose;
    watchedItems[8] = ConfigId_velocitySensitivity;
    watchedItems[9] = ConfigId_pressureSensitivity;
    watchedItems[10] = ConfigId_liftSensitivity;
    watchedItems[11] = ConfigId_fixedVelocity;
    watchedItems[12] = ConfigId_xTrackingMode;


    for (int i = 0; i < numWatched; i = i + 1) {
        watchedLast[i] = 0 - 1000;
    }

    for (int j = 0; j < 24; j = j + 1) {
        pressed[j] = 0;
        keyPressure[j] = 0;
        keyBend[j] = 8192;
        keyChannel[j] = -1;
        keyNote[j] = -1;
    }
}
